;; A board on your tailnet, reachable from anywhere, with nothing forwarded.
;;
;; WireGuard needs an inbound port, which means a port forward, a dynamic DNS
;; name, and a public address to forward from. Tailscale needs none of those:
;; nodes find each other through a coordination server, so a board behind
;; CGNAT or a locked-down router joins the same way as one with a static IP.
;; The cost is a dependency on that service, and a Go binary where WireGuard
;; is a kernel module.
;;
;; Size, because it is the first thing anyone asks: tailscaled and tailscale
;; together are tens of megabytes against a few hundred kilobytes for
;; wireguard-tools, since this carries its own WireGuard, its own DNS
;; handling and its own network stack. On a 120MB rootfs that is most of the
;; headroom, so images composing this want a bigger one.
;;
;; The interesting part is state. /var/lib/tailscale/tailscaled.state holds
;; the node key: lose it and the board is a new node next boot, which
;; reauthenticates, appears again in the admin console, and needs its routes
;; approving again. On a writable rootfs it survives reboots but not a
;; reflash, and on a read-only image it would need a partition of its own.
;; An appliance that is regenerated from a config file has nowhere natural to
;; put a secret it earned at runtime, which is worth saying out loud rather
;; than discovering later.
;;
;; TS_ROUTES in /etc/default/tailscale makes the board a subnet router, so
;; the other machines on its LAN are reachable from the tailnet without
;; installing anything on them. That is the whole point for a device like an
;; ESPHome relay: it never learns about the VPN at all.

(fragment feature:tailscale

  (doc "tailscaled, joined at boot, optionally routing its LAN")

  (requires
    (capability mmu))

  (buildroot
    ;; ca-certificates is selected by the package: without it the TLS
    ;; handshake to the coordination server fails, and the error looks like
    ;; a network problem rather than a missing trust store.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_TAILSCALE))

  (linux
    ;; tailscaled routes packets through /dev/net/tun. Without this the
    ;; daemon starts, fails to create the interface, and falls back to
    ;; userspace networking where it can reach out but nothing can route
    ;; through it - so a subnet router silently does not route.
    (at-least m CONFIG_TUN)

    ;; Advertising routes means forwarding, and forwarding for hosts that do
    ;; not know about the tunnel means NAT on the way back.
    (y CONFIG_NETFILTER)
    (y CONFIG_NF_NAT)
    (y CONFIG_IP_NF_IPTABLES)
    (y CONFIG_IP_NF_NAT)))
